 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Детектор лжи  (The Lie Detector)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "expert": "Эксперт",
  "fedya": "Федя"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "The chef is determined to find the traitor, so he has hired a lie-detector expert and set up a machine in his office. Fedya, the fish cook, is the first to be questioned. Here is the Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Заходи, Федя. Садись на стул.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Шеф, а что это такое?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это настоящий детектор лжи, Федя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я должен поймать крысу на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Отвечайте только «да» или «нет».",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Хорошо, это мне вполне понятно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Скажите, сегодня действительно среда?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да, сегодня среда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Ваше настоящее имя Фёдор?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты ходил в соседний ресторан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Нет, я никуда не ходил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Свободен. Позови сюда Сеню.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go through the questioning.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Заходи, Федя. Садись на стул.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Come in, Fedya. Sit down on the chair.\" The term for come in is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Заходи,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", built from the starting part for going inside. The action word for seat yourself is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Садись",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", built from an ending that means doing it to yourself.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Шеф, а что это такое?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Chef, what is this thing?\" The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "что это такое?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally translates to \"what is this such?\", a handy way to ask what something is.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это настоящий детектор лжи, Федя.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"This is a real lie detector, Fedya.\" The phrase for a lie drops a sound to become ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "лжи,",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning \"of a lie\". The whole phrase translates to \"detector of a lie\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я должен поймать крысу на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"I must catch the rat in the kitchen.\" The Russian expression for rat, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "крысу",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", is also slang for a traitor. The action word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "поймать",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " translates to \"to catch\", and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "должен",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the describing word for \"must\" when a man is speaking.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Отвечайте только «да» или «нет».",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Answer only yes or no.\" The action word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Отвечайте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is an instruction built from a polite ending. The Russian term for only is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "только",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", and the little word for or is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "или",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Хорошо, это мне вполне понятно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Fine, that is completely clear to me.\" The translation for completely, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "вполне",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", literally translates to \"in full\". The word for clear, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "понятно.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", needs no word for \"I\" when you say it is clear to you.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Скажите, сегодня действительно среда?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Tell me, is today really Wednesday?\" The Russian term for Wednesday, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "среда?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", is built from the phrase for middle, since it falls mid-week. The phrase for really is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "действительно",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да, сегодня среда.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Yes, today is Wednesday.\" Fedya repeats the day of the week, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "сегодня среда.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " with no action word, because the language leaves out the Russian expression for \"is\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Ваше настоящее имя Фёдор?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Is your real name Fyodor?\" The full name behind Fedya is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "Фёдор?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ". The describing word for real, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "настоящее",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", takes a matching ending for the Russian term for name, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "expert",
   "t": "имя",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Yes.\" Just ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Да.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", which is exactly what the expert asked for.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ты ходил в соседний ресторан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"Did you go to the neighboring restaurant?\" The action word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "ходил",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally translates to \"went and came back\". The describing word for neighboring, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "соседний",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", is built from the Russian term for neighbor.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Нет, я никуда не ходил.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"No, I did not go anywhere.\" The phrase ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "никуда не ходил.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally translates to \"nowhere not went\", which doubles the negative.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Свободен. Позови сюда Сеню.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "\"You are free to go. Call Senya in here.\" The Russian expression for free is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Свободен.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", used here as a short word for a man. The action word for call is ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Позови",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", and Senya turns into ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Сеню.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " as the target of the action. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Заходи, Федя. Садись на стул.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "fedya",
   "t": "Шеф, а что это такое?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это настоящий детектор лжи, Федя.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я должен поймать крысу на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
